      links: [],
      abstract: [
        'Alzheimer\'s disease (AD) progression trajectories vary significantly depending on the timing of treatment, making the prediction of future anatomical changes crucial. However, due to the subtle, localized, and heterogeneous nature of AD-related neurodegeneration, this remains a uniquely challenging task. While previous studies have improved predictive generation performance by integrating patient information, they primarily focus on generation, rely on 2D models, have limited support for fused patient data, or lack robust interval-aware capabilities. To address these limitations, we propose ADP-DiT 3D, a multimodal framework that performs progression generation and diagnostic classification simultaneously. We propose contribution-matched activation for balanced joint optimization of generation and classification, initializing the newly activated classification-loss weight from the current mean weighted contribution of the generative objectives within an uncertainty-weighted joint objective. We further incorporate 3D rotary positional embeddings and interval-time embeddings to model volumetric spatial relationships and temporal progression, respectively. Furthermore, we compress the CLIP text encoder to alleviate computational complexity. In progression generation on multi-cohort data, ADP-DiT 3D achieved an SSIM of 0.885 and a PSNR of 24.7, corresponding to 3.9% higher SSIM and 7.1% higher PSNR than the state-of-the-art model.'
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Null-Space Diffusion Restoration with Adaptive Uncertainty-Guided Fusion for Ultrasound Speckle Reduction',
      authors: ['Juneyong Lee', 'Jaeyoung Choi'],
      venue: 'IEEE Access',
      short: 'IEEE Access',
      detail: 'September 2026',
      status: 'published',
      period: '1st Semester 2026',
      printYear: '2026',
      tags: ['Ultrasound Speckle Reduction', 'Uncertainty-Guided Null-Space Diffusion', 'Adaptive Range-Null Reconstruction', 'Label-Free Diffusion Restoration'],
      links: [
        { label: 'DOI', url: 'https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3729696' },
        { label: 'IEEE Xplore', url: 'https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/11675827' },
        { label: 'Code', url: 'https://github.com/yousirong/UGNS' }
      ],
      abstract: [
        'Ultrasound B-mode imaging commonly suffers from speckle noise and artifacts, requiring a delicate balance between contrast, resolution, and preservation of anatomical structures. Although recently developed despeckling methods have achieved some progress, supervised learning approaches remain fundamentally limited by the ground truth paradox, which arises from the absence of noise-free, ground truth reference images in in vivo scenarios. Existing unsupervised diffusion-based methods typically enforce data consistency directly in the nonlinear log-compressed domain, which can disproportionately amplify background artifacts when mapped back to the envelope domain. To overcome these limitations, we propose an uncertainty-guided null-space diffusion (UGNS) framework, a novel label-free solution that enforces consistency correction on a stabilized positive-envelope proxy obtained via inverse log compression. The proposed UGNS introduces several technical novelties: (a) extraction of a structural prior in the stabilized envelope domain to produce a robust signal envelope that preserves anatomical structure, (b) development of an adaptive range-null reconstruction mechanism that uses an adaptive weight mask to preserve tissue regions via range-space projection, and (c) introduction of uncertainty-guided fusion in an adaptive way to mitigate sampling variability. Extensive and comparative experiments were conducted using the PICMUS benchmark and in vivo datasets. The results demonstrate that UGNS achieves competitive generalized contrast-to-noise ratio (gCNR) values across diverse datasets. In addition, UGNS effectively suppresses speckle noise while preserving fine spatial resolution.'
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Transmit-Conditioned Single-Pass Null-Space Diffusion for High-Quality Restoration of Low-Quality Ultrasound Images',
      authors: ['Juneyong Lee', 'Jae Young Choi'],
      venue: 'Journal of Korea Multimedia Society',
      short: 'J. Korea Multimedia Society',
      detail: 'Vol. 29, No. 8, pp. 1127-1142, August 2026',
      status: 'published',
      period: '1st Semester 2026',
      printYear: '2026',
      tags: ['Sparse-Transmit Ultrasound Restoration', 'Null-Space Diffusion', 'Single-Pass Inference', 'Transmit-Conditioned Diffusion'],
      links: [
        { label: 'DOI', url: 'https://doi.org/10.9717/kmms.2026.29.8.1127' },
        { label: 'Code', url: 'https://github.com/yousirong/TCNS' }
      ],
      abstract: [
        'Ultrasound B-mode imaging using sparse-transmit delay-and-sum (DAS) beamforming can improve acquisition efficiency, but suffers from reduced contrast, unstable speckle statistics, and loss of anatomical detail with fewer transmit events. In this paper, we propose a transmit-conditioned single-pass null-space diffusion model for restoring sparse-transmit ultrasound images toward a 75Tx DAS reference. The proposed model learns a paired conditional restoration distribution from 1Tx/11Tx DAS inputs and a transmit-level condition map to a 75Tx DAS target. The inference stage is designed so that the low-frequency structural component of the current clean estimate is accumulated as an exponential moving average prior in the wavelet range space, while the high-frequency speckle component is regenerated by the diffusion prior within the same DDIM trajectory. The proposed model preserves anatomical structure without relying on ensemble sampling or a separate prior trajectory, significantly reducing computational cost. Extensive experiments on both phantom and preclinical murine FSA datasets show that the proposed method considerably improves perceptual fidelity and speckle-level matching to the 75Tx reference while reducing over-smoothing, compared with recently developed denoising methods.'
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'ADP-DiT: Text-Guided Diffusion Transformer for Brain Image Generation in Alzheimer\'s Disease Progression',
      authors: ['Juneyong Lee', 'Geonwoo Baek', 'Ikbeom Jang', 'for the Alzheimer\'s Disease Neuroimaging Initiative'],
      note: 'Juneyong Lee and Geonwoo Baek contributed equally (co-first authors).',
      venue: 'Pattern Recognition: 28th International Conference (ICPR 2026), Lyon, France',
      short: 'ICPR 2026',
      detail: 'Proceedings Part III, pp. 301-317, August 2026',
      status: 'published',
      period: '2nd Semester 2025',
      printYear: '2026',
      tags: ['Alzheimer\'s Disease', 'Disease Progression', 'Text-Guided Image Generation', 'Diffusion Transformer'],
      links: [
        { label: 'DOI', url: 'https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-31654-7_21' },
        { label: 'Code', url: 'https://github.com/labhai/ADP-DiT' }
      ],
      abstract: [
        'Alzheimer\'s disease (AD) progresses heterogeneously across individuals, motivating subject-specific synthesis of follow-up magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) to support progression assessment. While Diffusion Transformers (DiT), an emerging transformer-based diffusion model, offer a scalable backbone for image synthesis, longitudinal AD MRI generation with clinically interpretable control over follow-up time and participant metadata remains underexplored.',
        'We present ADP-DiT, an interval-aware, clinically text-conditioned diffusion transformer for longitudinal AD MRI synthesis. ADP-DiT encodes follow-up interval together with multi-domain demographic, diagnostic (CN/MCI/AD), and neuropsychological information as a natural-language prompt, enabling time-specific control beyond coarse diagnostic stages. To inject this conditioning effectively, we use dual text encoders - OpenCLIP for vision-language alignment and T5 for richer clinical-language understanding. Their embeddings are fused into DiT through cross-attention for fine-grained guidance and adaptive layer normalization for global modulation. We further enhance anatomical fidelity by applying rotary positional embeddings to image tokens and performing diffusion in a pretrained SDXL-VAE latent space to enable efficient high-resolution reconstruction.',
        'On 3,321 longitudinal 3T T1-weighted scans from 712 participants (259,038 image slices), ADP-DiT achieves SSIM 0.8739 and PSNR 29.32 dB, improving over a DiT baseline by +0.1087 SSIM and +6.08 dB PSNR while capturing progression-related changes such as ventricular enlargement and shrinking hippocampus. These results suggest that integrating comprehensive, subject-specific clinical conditions with architectures can improve longitudinal AD MRI synthesis.'
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Lightweight Temporal Segment Network for Video Scene Understanding: Validation in Driver Assault Detection',
      authors: ['Juneyong Lee', 'Joon Kim', 'Junhui Park', 'Jongho Jo', 'Ikbeom Jang'],
      venue: 'Journal of KIISE (Korean Institute of Information Scientists and Engineers)',
      short: 'J. KIISE',
      detail: 'Vol. 51, No. 11, pp. 987-995, November 2024',
      status: 'published',
      period: '1st Semester 2024',
      printYear: '2024',
      tags: ['Temporal Segment Network', 'Lightweight CNN Architecture', 'Assault Recognition', 'Abnormal Behavior'],
      links: [
        { label: 'DOI', url: 'https://doi.org/10.5626/JOK.2024.51.11.987' },
        { label: 'Code', url: 'https://github.com/HUFSuperman/HUFS_SavingDriver' }
      ],
      abstract: [
        'There has been an increasing number of driver assaults in transportation such as taxis and buses in the past years. It can be especially difficult to respond quickly to assaults on drivers by drunks late at night. To address this issue, our research team proposes a lightweight CNN-based Temporal Segment Network (TSN) model, which can detect driver assaults by passengers in real time. The TSN model efficiently processes videos by sampling a small number of image frames and is divided into two streams for learning: one for spatial information processing and the other for temporal information processing. Convolutional neural networks are employed in each stream. In this research, we apply a lightweight CNN architecture, MobileOne, significantly reducing the model size while demonstrating improved accuracy even with limited computing resources. The model is expected to contribute to a rapid response and prevention of hazardous situations for drivers by being integrated into vehicular driver monitoring systems.'
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Research on Developing a Responsive Web Interface for Small Businesses Using React',
      authors: [],
      venue: 'Collaboration with PeopleCat Co., Ltd.',
      short: 'PeopleCat Co., Ltd.',
      detail: '',
      status: 'published',
      period: '2nd Semester 2023',
      printYear: '2023',
      printGroup: 'activities',
      tags: ['Responsive Web Interface', 'React', 'Asynchronous Processing', 'Information Architecture Design'],
      links: [
        { label: 'Code', url: 'https://github.com/yousirong/CAPSTONE_AWS' }
      ],
      abstract: [
        'Although large corporations have established sales databases for efficient sales activities, small-scale entrepreneurs need a system that provides an interface that anyone can easily manage, offering diverse and complex information. This paper proposes the use of React technology, which can also operate on crawling servers that automatically collect store information. Furthermore, it suggests the development of a responsive web user interface that can be conveniently used in both mouse and keyboard-based desktop environments as well as touch-based smartphone environments, utilizing React technology for the effective delivery of various data. The proposed methods anticipate rapid retrieval of large volumes of data due to the adoption of asynchronous function processing in React technology, an improvement over traditional JavaScript methods.'
      ]
    }
